⚠️ **IMPORTANT: `needsNameResolution` is named opposite to what it returns**

`js/fetchParentContextToInput.js:192`:

```javascript
/** Guard: only non-system, non-customfield names need a tracker lookup. */
function needsNameResolution(fieldName) {
    return !fieldName || isSystemField(fieldName) || fieldName.indexOf('customfield_') === 0;
}
```

The name and the JSDoc both state the opposite of the boolean's polarity: `needsNameResolution('summary')` returns `true`, yet `summary` needs **no** tracker lookup. The caller only reads correctly with a double mental negation:

```javascript
if (needsNameResolution(fieldName) || !shouldResolve || !tracker) {
    return fieldName; // pass through
}
```

Every future reader (and the new unit test itself, whose assertions must explain "falsy needs no lookup" against a `true` result) will trip on this. Since the helper is now exported as module API (`module.exports` includes it) alongside the established `resolveParentContextConfig`/`buildDefaultFields` test-export pattern, please rename it to state the actual predicate — e.g. `isPassThroughField(fieldName)` or `shouldSkipResolution(fieldName)` — and flip the JSDoc ("a falsy, system, or already-resolved `customfield_*` name is returned as-is without a tracker lookup"). Pure rename, no behavior change; the 12 new assertions in `test_fetchParentContextToInput.js` pin the semantics either way.

